      "body": "## Overview\n\nThis is an NVIDIA-developed specialized decode of Muse Spark Open (NVFP4, DSpark speculative decoding) for GB300 that gives 1.5x single-stream decode throughput, in the form of the Kimi K3 specialized decode (#61867).\n\n- Tensor parallel 4, 8 or 16 with expert parallelism (EP = TP); at TP8 the routed experts run tensor-parallel inside the executor.\n- DSpark verification with 7 draft tokens, including the draft model's forward and its Markov sampling loop.\n- Any context length up to `--max-model-len`, including sliding-window layers.\n\nIt is opt-in with `--additional-config '{\"decode_backend\": \"latency_specialized\"}'`; the default, `auto`, keeps the stock decode. Changes are localized to `vllm/models/muse_spark/` (the executor in `nvidia/specialized_decode/`, the model hooks, and the DSpark draft model, which implements the speculator's `sample_sequential` hook) plus three CUDA C ops in `csrc/muse_spark/` and their registration (`CMakeLists.txt`, `csrc/ops.h`, `csrc/torch_bindings.cpp`, `vllm/_custom_ops.py`, additions only). No other shared vLLM code changes.\n\n## Claims\n\nSingle-stream decode (ISL 1, OSL 1000, concurrency 1, DSpark K 7 with forced acceptance length 6) on GB300 NVL72 trays, ours and stock vLLM on one allocation per row:\n\n- **TP8+EP8: 1.53x** (698.60 -> 1072.14 tok/s/user)\n- **gsm8k matches stock** within one standard error at TP8+EP8 (1253 vs 1255 of 1319).\n\n## Validation\n\n### Correctness Testing\n\n| Task | Stock TP8+EP8 | Ours TP8+EP8 |\n|---|---|---|\n| gsm8k 5-shot, flexible-extract (n 1319) | 0.9515 +- 0.0059 | 0.9500 +- 0.0060 | | gsm8k 5-shot, strict-match (n 1319) | 0.9522 +- 0.0059 | 0.9492 +- 0.0060 |\n\n`--max-model-len 32768`, adaptive verification off, block rejection, concurrency 1, both arms on one allocation. Paired, U - S is -2 questions (flexible-extract; exact McNemar p 0.83). Greedy outputs at TP4, TP8 and TP16 are identical across server starts.\n\n### Performance Validation\n\n\n<img width=\"600\" height=\"420\" alt=\"specdec-speedup\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/aa34414e-4e1f-4b14-9077-1a314fab72a2\" />\n\n\nDSpark speculative decoding only (the executor serves the verify step); median interactivity (1000 / median TPOT) over 200 requests, each row's two arms on one allocation.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Commands and setup</summary>\n\n```bash\n# Server: each tray runs one vllm serve with --node-rank k (rank 0 serves the API, the others add --headless)\nvllm serve meta-models/Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B-NVFP4 \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size <8|16> --enable-expert-parallel --nnodes <2|4> --node-rank <k> --master-addr <rank0> \\\n  --max-model-len <2048|1048576> --reasoning-parser muse --tool-call-parser muse --enable-auto-tool-choice \\\n  --numa-bind --numa-bind-nodes 0 0 1 1 \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"dspark\",\"model\":\"<draft>\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":7,\n    \"draft_sample_method\":\"probabilistic\",\"rejection_sample_method\":\"synthetic\",\n    \"synthetic_acceptance_length\":6,\"enable_adaptive_verification\":false}' \\\n  --additional-config '{\"decode_backend\":\"latency_specialized\"}'   # ours only\n\n# Client\nvllm bench serve --backend openai --endpoint /v1/completions --model meta-models/Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B-NVFP4 \\\n  --dataset-name random --random-input-len 1 --random-output-len 1000 --random-range-ratio 0 --ignore-eos --seed 0 \\\n  --max-concurrency 1 --num-prompts 200 --num-warmups 2 --percentile-metrics ttft,tpot,itl,e2el\n```\n\nBoth arms: vLLM main at 1fd6337e22 (stock) and the same with this change, CUDA 13.0, torch 2.14.0, FlashInfer 0.7.2.post1 (vLLM's pin), the model with its two BF16 expert layers quantized to NVFP4 locally, no `--linear-backend`, the default FlashInfer all-reduce backend.\n\n</details>\n\n## Details\n\n### vLLM Integration\n\nThe Muse Spark model builds a `SpecializedDecode` after its weights load (`nvidia/model_state.py`, using the ModelState protocol and `initialize_kv_cache()` as #61867 does) and calls it from `forward()`. It serves the verify step of one request (1 + 7 draft tokens) captured into FULL CUDA graphs; when `plan()` returns None (prefill, mixed batches, several requests, partial drafts, eager or PIECEWISE steps) the stock forward runs. `unsupported()` checks the deployment at startup and names the first requirement it fails. The lm_head, `compute_logits` and the attention backend's metadata and KV cache stay shared.\n\nThe executor runs, per layer, the input boundary, the attention (fused QKV + QK-norm + RoPE + KV insert, decode attention fused with the output norm and gate, o_proj), the attention boundary and the MoE (FP32 router, routing that also writes the routed experts' metadata, pooler GEMM with a fused all-gather + norm + NVFP4 quantization, the TRT-LLM-gen routed experts, finalize, unpooler GEMM), each block a provider in `specialized_decode/runtime/`. The fused all-reduce boundaries sum in the order of the stock FlashInfer all-reduce backend the deployment resolves (MNNVL or TRT-LLM), so TP4 needs vLLM's FlashInfer all-reduce. The draft model runs its forward and, through the DSpark speculator's `sample_sequential` hook, the Markov loop over a vocab-parallel Markov head (`runtime/draft.py`, `runtime/markov.py`).\n\nKernels are in `specialized_decode/cutedsl/` (CuTe DSL) and `csrc/muse_spark/` (CUDA C: the QKV front, the decode attention and the routing). FlashInfer is used only from `runtime/flashinfer_ops.py`: the public routed experts ops, and on NVFP4 layers the runner below the public op, fed by the package's routing metadata, with the expert-TP tactic pinned per trtllm-gen cubin package (the op's default under any other). Kernels that reuse FlashInfer code are adapted from its source at a public sha, each header carrying the Apache-2.0 notice.\n\nUnit and kernel tests are kept out of this PR, as in #61867 (available on request; 2735 passed on one GB300 tray).\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide. </details>\n\nAI assistance was used to write this change, its tests (kept locally) and this description, and to run the measurements.\n\n\n\n## Overview\n\n\n\n## Claims\n\n\n\n## Validation\n\n\n\n## Details\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [ ] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
